// ============================================================================
// boom.cm — adversarial stress fixture for Checkmate (CME) error recovery
// ============================================================================
//
// The evil twin of test.cm. Where test.cm must produce ZERO diagnostics,
// boom.cm is broken ON PURPOSE: every recoverable failure mode of the lexer,
// the newline-strip pass and the parser appears here at least once.
//
// The contract under test (see crates/cme-compiler/src/parser.rs):
//   1. The parser NEVER stops and NEVER panics — it always consumes the file.
//   2. Every broken region becomes an `Invalid` node carrying its
//      `SyntaxError`, and the same diagnostic lands in the errors vec.
//   3. Statements that are recognizable survive even when their pieces are
//      broken: `int i = str wow how` still yields VarDecl{Int, "i", Invalid},
//      so a future LSP still sees that `i` is an Int.
//   4. Source text that was never typed becomes a ZERO-WIDTH `Invalid`
//      ("missing node") — exactly where an LSP completion would insert.
//   5. Healthy sibling statements are untouched by their broken neighbors —
//      including the line BELOW a broken one: the strip pass keeps the
//      newline after a dangling operator/type when the next line starts a
//      statement, so recovery cannot fuse two lines into one.
//
// Run it (the CLI exits 1 — that is the point; zero diagnostics is a bug):
//   cargo run --features cli -- ast boom.cm
//   cargo run --features cli -- lex boom.cm
//
// The Rust test `boom_cm_stress_fixture` (cme-compiler/src/lib.rs) pins the
// exact outcomes documented below. File layout notes:
//   - ALL comments and blank lines are transparent to the parser — except
//     that a `"` inside a comment re-opens an unterminated string literal
//     started in the CODE of the same line (see §9 for the documented case).
//   - Section 16 is kept LAST on purpose: an unclosed `(` makes every
//     following newline insignificant, so it would swallow the whole rest
//     of the file (see §16 for why that is the honest current behavior).
//   - One diagnostic per marked line unless noted otherwise. Braces are
//     lex errors; each `}` on its own code line is recorded once.
//
// Sections:
//   1.  Broken code as the very first statement
//   2.  Flagship: broken initializers, surviving declarations
//   3.  Missing pieces: zero-width Invalid (missing `=` / initializer)
//   4.  Healthy siblings survive broken neighbors
//   5.  Assignment / compound-assignment RHS failures
//   6.  Garbage statement heads
//   7.  Missing / invalid variable names
//   8.  Bare identifiers without an operator
//   9.  Lexer errors: bad chars, unterminated strings, `1.2.3`
//   10. Unbalanced closing parenthesis (strip-pass recovery)
//   11. Empty and broken (but balanced) parenthesized groups
//   12. Operator-grammar violations (mixed &&/||, chained comparisons)
//   13. Continuation lines and multi-line paren groups gone wrong
//   14. Trailing garbage after a complete statement (same-line loss)
//   15. The LSP simulation: half-broken program that still knows everything
//   16. EOF: the unclosed parenthesis that swallows the world (kept last)
// ============================================================================


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Broken code as the very first statement — no healthy context needed
//    before recovery kicks in. One diagnostic, then a healthy statement that
//    references the survivor.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

int first = }                    // 1 lex diagnostic; VarDecl{Int, "first", Invalid "}"}
infer second = first + 1         // healthy


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Flagship — the motivating case. Every declared type survives with an
//    Invalid initializer covering the skipped region; one diagnostic per
//    line ("expected an expression, but found <token>").
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

int i = str wow how              // VarDecl{Int, "i", Invalid "str wow how"}
float f = }                      // 1 lex diagnostic; VarDecl{Float, "f", Invalid "}"}
str s = *                        // VarDecl{Str, "s", Invalid "*"}
bool b = %                       // VarDecl{Bool, "b", Invalid "%"}
infer v = +                      // VarDecl{Infer, "v", Invalid "+"}


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Missing pieces — nothing was typed where an expression belongs, so the
//    Invalid is ZERO-WIDTH at exactly the position an LSP would complete at.
//    The strip pass keeps every newline here (the next line always starts
//    with a type keyword), so no line fuses with its neighbor.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

int count =                      // VarDecl{Int, "count", Invalid zero-width}
int k                            // VarDecl{Int, "k", Invalid zero-width} ("expected `=`")
int k2 5                         // Stmt::Invalid ("expected `=`, but found integer literal `5`")
infer draft =                    // VarDecl{Infer, "draft", Invalid zero-width}


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Healthy siblings survive — broken neighbors change nothing around them,
//    even three failures in a row.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

int left = 1                     // healthy
int middle = str boom boom       // VarDecl{Int, "middle", Invalid "str boom boom"}
int right = 2                    // healthy
int brokenA = str a              // broken
int brokenB = str b              // broken
int brokenC = str c              // broken
int after = 3                    // healthy


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. Assignment and compound assignment with broken RHS — the target survives
//    in every form. `score *=` proves the newline after a dangling operator
//    survives when the next line starts a declaration; `score /= )` stacks
//    TWO recovery layers: the strip pass reports the unbalanced `)` AND the
//    parser plants a zero-width Invalid.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

int score = 10                   // healthy
score = str nope                 // Assign{"score", Invalid "str nope"}
score += }                       // 1 lex diagnostic; CompoundAssign{+, Invalid "}"}
score -= str y z                 // CompoundAssign{-, Invalid "str y z"}
score *=                         // CompoundAssign{*, Invalid zero-width}
int fence = 20                   // healthy — the newline after `*=` was NOT eaten
score /= )                       // CompoundAssign{/, Invalid zero-width} + 1 strip diagnostic
score %= 2                       // healthy


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 6. Garbage statement heads — nothing recognizable at the head, so the whole
//    line becomes one Stmt::Invalid ("expected a type or assignment target").
//    `) straggler` is dropped by the STRIP pass before the parser ever sees
//    it: one diagnostic, zero statements. The `}` line is likewise dropped
//    as a lexer-level brace error: one diagnostic, zero statements.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

}                                // 1 lex diagnostic (brace error); no statement
* 5                              // Stmt::Invalid (Star)
true                             // Stmt::Invalid (KwTrue — keywords are not statements)
42                               // Stmt::Invalid (IntLit(42))
+ + +                            // Stmt::Invalid (Plus)
) straggler                      // NO statement — strip-level drop, 1 diagnostic


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 7. Missing / invalid variable names — the declaration head cannot even be
//    recognized, so the statement becomes Stmt::Invalid (the name itself was
//    never learned, which is the honest outcome). The bare `float` keeps its
//    newline (a dangling type keyword can never continue a line).
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

int = 5                          // Stmt::Invalid ("expected a variable name, but found `=`")
int return = 1                   // Stmt::Invalid (KwReturn is a keyword, not a name)
int 5 = 1                        // Stmt::Invalid (IntLit(5))
float                            // Stmt::Invalid ("expected a variable name, but found end of statement")


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 8. Bare identifiers — an identifier alone cannot form a statement without
//    an assignment operator.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

lonely                           // Stmt::Invalid ("expected an assignment operator, but found end of statement")
x y                              // Stmt::Invalid ("...but found identifier `y`")


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 9. Lexer errors — the lexer records the bad region and skips to the next
//    newline, so the damaged line degrades but the FILE continues.
//    NOTE on `str oops`: the comment below deliberately avoids `"` characters
//    — a quote inside a comment after an unterminated string in the code
//    re-opens the literal (the string regex wins over the comment skip), a
//    documented lexer sharp edge.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

int cursed = 1 @ 2               // 1 lex diagnostic; the 2 is dropped; VarDecl{Int, cursed, 1} survives
@                                // 1 lex diagnostic; no statement at all
@ $                              // 2 lex diagnostics — every bad character is recorded, not swallowed
str oops = "unterminated         // 1 lex + 1 parse; VarDecl{Str, oops, Invalid zero-width}
infer fragile = 1.2.3            // 1 lex diagnostic; VarDecl{Infer, fragile, FloatLit(1.2)} survives
infer afterAt = @ 5              // 1 lex + 1 parse; VarDecl{Infer, afterAt, Invalid zero-width}
                                 // (the zero-width diagnostic reports at the next surviving newline,
                                 //  because blank/comment lines collapse into one newline token)
int huge = 99999999999999999999999
                                 // 1 lex + 1 parse; a 23-digit literal overflows i64 — the digit
                                 // run itself is the invalid token (no panic!), the declaration
                                 // survives as VarDecl{Int, huge, Invalid zero-width}


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 10. Unbalanced CLOSING parenthesis — the strip pass reports it and skips to
//     the next newline; whatever preceded it on the line still parses.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

infer q = 1 + 2)                 // 1 strip diagnostic; VarDecl{Infer, q, 1 + 2} survives
int q2 = (3 * 4)) extra dropped  // 1 strip diagnostic; VarDecl{Int, q2, (3 * 4)} survives


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 11. Empty and broken (but BALANCED) parenthesized groups — recovery happens
//     inside the statement, which survives whole.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

infer empty = ()                 // VarDecl{Infer, empty, Invalid "()"}
infer mixed = (str boom)         // VarDecl{Infer, mixed, Invalid "(str boom)"}


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 12. Operator-grammar violations — the violation is diagnosed mid-expression
//     at its real span: the mixed &&/|| diagnostic spans the offending
//     unparenthesized operand (recorded by both validation passes) and the RHS
//     survives as a real tree; the chained-comparison diagnostic points at the
//     second operator and its RHS becomes Invalid.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

infer mixedBad = alive && ready || armed   // RHS survives as a real (alive && ready) || armed tree ("mixed && and || require parentheses" spans "alive && ready")
infer chained = 1 < 2 < 3                  // Invalid covers "1 < 2 < 3" ("comparisons are non-associative")


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 13. Continuations and multi-line paren groups gone wrong. `int cont = 1 +`
//     ends dangling before a NEW DECLARATION — the newline survives, so cont
//     gets its own Invalid and boom2 keeps existing with a missing `=`.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

int cont = 1 +
str boom2                        // VarDecl{Int, cont, Invalid "1 +"} + VarDecl{Str, boom2, Invalid zero-width}

infer multi = (
    1 +
    str boom3
)                                // VarDecl{Infer, multi, Invalid spanning the whole group}

int afterMulti = 4               // healthy


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 14. Trailing garbage after a COMPLETE statement — the statement survives
//     with a diagnostic; same-line recovery is line-granular, so `int late`
//     sharing the damaged line is dropped (documented limitation), while the
//     next line is untouched.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

int ok = 1 }                     // 1 lex diagnostic (brace); VarDecl{Int, ok, 1} survives
int ok2 = 2 } int late = 3       // 1 lex diagnostic (brace); int late = 3 is dropped with the damaged line
int survivor = 5                 // healthy


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 15. The LSP simulation — a symbol-table pass walking this tree still sees
//     hp: Int, mp: Int, total, name, flag, brokenFlag: Bool. Completions keep
//     working on the broken lines because the declarations survived.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

int hp = 100                     // healthy
int mp = str not even close      // VarDecl{Int, mp, Invalid "str not even close"}
infer total = hp + 1             // healthy — references the survivor above
str name = "player"              // healthy
bool flag = mp == 1              // healthy syntactically
bool brokenFlag = str no && true // VarDecl{Bool, brokenFlag, Invalid "str no && true"}


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 16. EOF — the unclosed parenthesis. KEPT LAST: an unclosed `(` makes every
//     following newline insignificant (they are stripped as inside parens),
//     so everything after this line would be swallowed into one statement.
//     Placed at the end, the damage is contained: two diagnostics (strip +
//     parse, both pointing at EOF) and a surviving declaration whose Invalid
//     covers the whole "(1 + 2" region. No trailing newline: this is the
//     exact byte stream an LSP sees while the user is still typing.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

infer unterminated = (1 + 2
